In Arduino, you can use the `millis()` function to create a timer. The `millis()` function returns the number of milliseconds since the Arduino board began running the current program. You can use this value to track the time elapsed and execute code at specific intervals.

Here's an example of how to use a timer in Arduino:
```
unsigned long previousMillis = 0;
unsigned long interval = 1000; //interval in milliseconds

void loop() {
  unsigned long currentMillis = millis();
  if (currentMillis - previousMillis >= interval) {
    //save the last time you blinked the LED
    previousMillis = currentMillis;
    //put your main code here, to run every interval milliseconds
  }
}
```
In this example, the code inside the if statement will execute every 1000 milliseconds (1 second). You can adjust the value of the `interval` variable to change the timer interval.

You can also use the `delay()` function to create a timer, but it's important to note that `delay()` will block the execution of the main loop until the delay is complete. This means that any code that comes after the `delay()` function will not execute until the delay is over. In contrast, using `millis()` allows you to continue executing the main loop while the timer is running.

So, in summary, to use a timer in Arduino, you can use the `millis()` function to track the time elapsed and execute code at specific intervals. This is a common approach for creating non-blocking timers in Arduino.
